.TH PAX 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
pax \- portable archive interchange
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pax
.RB [ \-cv ]
.RI [ \-f " archive" ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.br
.B pax \-r
.RB [ \-cdkuv ]
.RI [ \-f " archive" ]
.RI [ pattern ...]
.br
.B pax \-w
.RB [ \-dv ]
.RI [ \-f " archive" ]
.RI [ \-x " format" ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B pax \-r \-w
.RB [ \-dkuv ]
.RI [ file ...]
.I directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pax
reads and writes file archives and, in copy mode, copies whole
directory trees, in one of four modes selected by
.BR \-r " and " \-w :
.TP
.I list mode
(neither \fB\-r\fR nor \fB\-w\fR given) Write the name of each archive
member matching a
.I pattern
operand (or every member, with none given) to standard output.
.TP
.I read mode
(\fB\-r\fR given) Extract each archive member matching a
.I pattern
operand (or every member) into the current directory hierarchy.
.TP
.I write mode
(\fB\-w\fR given) Write each
.I file
operand (recursing into directories) to a newly created archive.
.TP
.I copy mode
(both \fB\-r\fR and \fB\-w\fR given) Copy each
.I file
operand (recursing into directories) directly into
.IR directory ,
without creating an archive at all.
.PP
Two archive formats are supported: ustar (the default for write mode)
and the octet-oriented ("odc") variant of cpio, selected with
.BR "\-x cpio" .
Both are auto-detected on read, regardless of which format wrote
them. The pax interchange format's own extended-header records (used
by other implementations to carry pathnames, sizes, or timestamps too
large for a plain ustar or cpio field) are not implemented; a member
that would require one is refused with a diagnostic at write time
rather than silently truncated, and
.RB \(lq "\-x pax" \(rq
itself is refused as an unimplemented format.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-w
Select read, write, or (both together) copy mode, as described above.
.TP
.BI \-f " archive"
The archive's pathname. In list or read mode, defaults to standard
input; in write mode, defaults to standard output. Ignored in copy
mode, which has no archive.
.TP
.BI \-x " format"
In write mode, selects the output archive format:
.B ustar
(the default) or
.BR cpio .
Any other value, including
.BR pax ,
is refused.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose. In read, write, and copy mode, writes the pathname of each
file processed to standard error as it is processed. In list mode,
produces an
.B ls
\-l\-style listing (type, mode, size, date, name, and, for a symbolic
link, its target) instead of a bare name.
.TP
.B \-d
For a directory named directly on the command line as a
.I file
operand (write and copy mode), archive or copy only the directory
entry itself, not its contents.
.TP
.B \-k
In read or copy mode, never overwrite a file that already exists at
the destination; the corresponding archive member is skipped instead.
.TP
.B \-u
In read or copy mode, skip restoring a member whose recorded
modification time is not newer than an existing file already at the
destination.
.TP
.B \-c
In list or read mode, match every member
.I except
those matched by a
.I pattern
operand, instead of only those matched by one.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I pattern
A shell glob pattern selecting archive members by name, for list and
read mode. A pattern containing none of
.RB \(lq * \(rq ,
.RB \(lq ? \(rq ,
or
.RB \(lq [ \(rq
additionally matches as a directory-prefix: a plain pattern
.RB \(lq usr \(rq
selects both a member literally named
.B usr
and every member whose name begins
.RB \(lq usr/ \(rq .
With no
.I pattern
operands, every member matches.
.TP
.I file
A pathname to archive or copy (write and copy mode); a directory
operand is recursed into unless
.B \-d
is given. With no
.I file
operands in write mode, a list of pathnames, one per line, is read
from standard input instead.
.TP
.I directory
The destination directory for copy mode; required, and must already
exist.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
On extraction (read or copy mode), a member pathname that is absolute
or contains a
.RB \(lq .. \(rq
path component is refused with a diagnostic and skipped, rather than
allowed to write outside the destination \(em a safety check beyond
what the format itself requires.
.PP
File ownership (user and group) is never restored on extraction: this
platform has no real multi-user ownership model, so there is nothing
meaningful to restore. Permission bits are restored as archived,
subject to the usual umask; modification time is restored via a
timestamp update after a regular file's data is written. Hard links
are never detected while walking
.I file
operands in write or copy mode (every source file is archived or
copied as an independent full copy, regardless of how many names it
has); on read, a ustar hard-link member is honored by linking to its
already-extracted target, but cpio's own hard-link convention is not
recognized and round-trips as independent copies instead. A FIFO,
character, block, or socket file encountered while walking a
.I file
operand is refused with a diagnostic and skipped; extracting a FIFO or
device-special member fails on this platform, which has no real
backing for either.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand was processed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid or unimplemented option, a missing or
unreadable archive, a refused or failed member, or a failure while
walking or writing a
.I file
operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ar (1),
.BR ls (1)
